# State & Lifecycle

State is an object that holds data that may change over time. When state changes, React re-renders the component.

### useState Hook
```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  return (
    <div>
      <p>You clicked {count} times</p>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Lifecycle
Components have a lifecycle: Mounting, Updating, and Unmounting. In functional components, we use `useEffect` to handle lifecycle events.

# The useEffect Hook

`useEffect` lets you perform side effects in functional components, such as data fetching, subscriptions, or manually changing the DOM.

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `You clicked ${count} times`;
}, [count]); // Only re-run if count changes
```

### Cleaning up an effect
If your effect returns a function, React will run it when it is time to clean up (e.g., when the component unmounts).

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const timer = setInterval(() => console.log('tick'), 1000);
  return () => clearInterval(timer);
}, []);
```

# Context API

Context provides a way to pass data through the component tree without having to pass props down manually at every level (prop drilling).

```jsx
const ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');

function App() {
  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value="dark">
      <Toolbar />
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

# Custom Hooks

Custom Hooks are JavaScript functions whose names start with "use" and that may call other Hooks. They allow you to reuse stateful logic between components.

```jsx
function useWindowWidth() {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(window.innerWidth);
  useEffect(() => {
    const handleResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
    window.addEventListener('resize', handleResize);
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handleResize);
  }, []);
  return width;
}
```
